WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.800
Your language will grow if you
can access authentic content.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.700
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here today
and today I want to talk about

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.460
authenticity in language learning.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.330
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.830
on the be for notification.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.670
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:21.540
I do appreciate it.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.150
So what do I mean by authenticity?

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:30.044
When we learn a language, when I learn
a language, and again, I'll quote from

00:00:30.534 --> 00:00:34.515
Stephen Krashen, where we had our, uh, we
had a very interesting discussion he...

00:00:34.515 --> 00:00:38.415
and you can see it here at YouTube,
a live stream a few days ago.

00:00:38.475 --> 00:00:44.205
And he talked about the importance of
meaningful communication, meaningful.

00:00:44.415 --> 00:00:48.644
So if you have a communication where
you are forcing yourself to talk

00:00:48.644 --> 00:00:52.364
about the weather, uh, or you're
listening to things of no interest to

00:00:52.364 --> 00:00:54.735
you, those are not meaningful to you.

00:00:55.125 --> 00:01:00.505
You wanna perhaps be choosing something
of interest because that something

00:01:00.525 --> 00:01:04.665
of interest is going to carry the
language into your brain because it's

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.405
meaningful because it's authentic.

00:01:07.005 --> 00:01:13.815
And I've often said that one of the faults
that many language learners exhibit is

00:01:13.815 --> 00:01:19.455
this unwillingness to leave the comfort
of the starter book, the learner language.

00:01:20.054 --> 00:01:22.155
To some extent it's unavoidable.

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:25.575
When I start into a language,
I do my mini stories.

00:01:25.575 --> 00:01:27.345
I spend three months on the mini stories.

00:01:27.345 --> 00:01:28.365
I listen to them often.

00:01:28.515 --> 00:01:29.985
There's lots of repetition.

00:01:29.985 --> 00:01:33.450
They are not authentic but
they have a lot of repetition.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.220
You need that to get started.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.820
That's kind of like the springboard,
you bounce two or three times on

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:43.770
this diving board, but once you're
up in the air, once you're, you know,

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:49.860
launched, you've gotta go find authentic
content and your language will grow

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.220
if you can access authentic content.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.110
And I mentioned to Dr.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:58.530
Krashen in our study, or in our
conversation rather that I've never

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:04.350
understood why in schools here in Canada,
these schools won't offer a language if

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:10.455
they don't have a certified teacher for
that language, as if only the teacher

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.835
can provide explanations on the language,
content in the language and so forth.

00:02:15.255 --> 00:02:20.445
The reality is the internet
is full of authentic content.

00:02:21.075 --> 00:02:25.035
Uh, I, for my Arabic, I've found this,
I've mentioned before this Jordanian

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.675
cartoon series, which I listen to.

00:02:26.915 --> 00:02:28.275
I listen to podcasts.

00:02:28.635 --> 00:02:32.805
I listen to material on, uh, I'm doing
one right now, a podcast in Persian

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:40.260
uh, Iranian identity on, uh, the, uh,
Islamic revolution in 1979, another one.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.510
These are all authentic materials.

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:43.890
So I am dis...

00:02:43.895 --> 00:02:49.020
sort of learning things of interest to
me in the language that's authentic.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.370
That's how we learn.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:56.310
There's no reason why in a school,
if they had a, a person familiar with

00:02:56.490 --> 00:03:01.005
how the internet can be used to learn
language, people could be accessing

00:03:01.035 --> 00:03:06.885
authentic content at their level cartoons
for kids, whatever the case may be.

00:03:07.395 --> 00:03:12.375
And therefore these kids at a school
without having a certified teacher for

00:03:12.375 --> 00:03:18.345
let's say Spanish or Portuguese could be
studying those materials at the school.

00:03:18.525 --> 00:03:23.600
As long as the teacher or language
coach was familiar with what's

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.280
available and maybe there would be a
network of, of teachers who would be

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.450
helping each other find this kind of
material, authentic material at an

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:37.820
appropriate level so that, uh, kids
who are motivated to learn will learn.

00:03:38.325 --> 00:03:42.645
Because accessing authentic
material is extremely motivating.

00:03:42.825 --> 00:03:45.555
I also mentioned in our
conversation with Dr.

00:03:45.555 --> 00:03:49.875
Krashen that in Canada, and this may
not be unique to Canada, but kids

00:03:49.875 --> 00:03:52.365
are almost forced to learn French.

00:03:52.545 --> 00:03:55.185
You know, French is a,
an official language.

00:03:55.454 --> 00:03:59.025
So you're asking kids to learn
French because it's, because

00:03:59.325 --> 00:04:00.495
it's an official language.

00:04:00.495 --> 00:04:02.715
It's, it's what, their
civic duty to learn French?

00:04:02.895 --> 00:04:04.665
That's not very motivating.

00:04:04.845 --> 00:04:11.100
However, if you can find authentic
content of interest to all these

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.360
different learners, and their interest
may vary there might be many different

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.800
languages that they're interested
in that with that kind of material

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.690
they are going to learn better.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:27.510
So I think this idea of authenticity,
authenticity in the materials that we

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:33.710
use and authentic typically is going
to mean aimed at the native speaker,

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.410
but it can be children's cartoons.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.300
It can be a cooking show.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:44.390
It can be, um, things on politics or
history or literature or short stories,

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:46.340
whatever it might be: authentic.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:50.900
And with the kinds of tools that
are available today, such as online

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:57.359
dictionaries, MP3 players, uh,
LingQ and, and more the ability

00:04:57.359 --> 00:05:01.169
to access authentic material is
much, much better than it ever was.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:05.159
It used to be you would have to
find, you know, a reader with a

00:05:05.164 --> 00:05:09.330
glossary and hopefully not too
difficult and a graded reader and,

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.280
and gradually work your way up.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:17.790
But nowadays it's possible to access
genuinely interesting, authentic

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.410
material much, much earlier.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.830
And so I think we should
aim for authenticity.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:28.800
I think language teachers, independent
teachers, online teachers at school should

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:34.020
be looking for ways that they can help
their learners access authentic material.

00:05:34.025 --> 00:05:38.160
And I think that is going to lead
to better results than keeping them

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.060
in, uh, traditional a sort of a
traditional textbook environment.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.100
Thank you for listening.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:45.100
Bye for now.

